Even more intriguing is the dipole response to the electric field of Fig. 2(b). Figure 2(d) presents the dipole pattern on the bottom layer right after \(t=(n+1/4)T\), and the in-plane dipole components form an antivortex. Specifically, dipoles in the horizontal "wing" region (\(3\leq|x-40|\leq 7\), \(36\leq y\leq 44\)) exhibit minor \(p_{z}\) and major \(p_{x}\) components pointing away from the center and align with the local horizontal electric field; when they move closer toward the center along \(x\) direction, dipoles at the edges of the wing gradually reorient along \(y\) direction; this gradually transitions to the vertical "wing" region (\(37\leq x\leq 43\), \(5\leq|y-40|\leq 9\)), where \(p_{x,y}\) primarily align along \(y\) direction pointing toward the center. Interestingly, with tiny local \(\mathbf{E}(\mathbf{r})\) in the vertical "wing" region, the development of \(p_{y}\) should be regarded as a nonlocal response, originating from dipole-dipole interaction and depolarization effect rather than the local \(\mathbf{p}(\mathbf{r})\cdot\mathbf{E}(\mathbf{r})\) coupling [85; 86]. Note that in both Figs. 2(c) and (d), \(p_{z}\) remains negative throughout the plane, indicating that no _topological_ phase transition occurs between the initial down-poled monodomain and the formed vortex ring or antivortex.

The polar antivortex in Fig. 2(d) with notable \(p_{y}\) is different from electric fields in Fig. 2(b), which only have \(E_{x}\) components. To elucidate the origin of the antivortex formation, we plot the distribution of the electric bound charge density \(\rho_{\rm b}(\mathbf{r})=-\nabla\cdot\mathbf{P}(\mathbf{r})\), which arises from the spatially nonuniform polarization. In general, a large bound charge can greatly increase the electrostatic energy, causing instability in the domain [87; 88; 6]; such instability can be mitigated with the self-organized polarization pattern bearing minimal divergence, effectively neutralizing \(\rho_{\rm b}\)[89; 90]. Figure 3(a) presents the bound charge component \(\rho_{\rm b,x}\) contributed by \(p_{x}\) at \(t=(n+1/4)T\), manifesting as large negative values at the layer center. The pronounced \(\rho_{\rm b,x}\) is associated with the intrinsically unstable tail-to-tail \(p_{x}\) pattern dictated by \(E_{x}\), whereas the presence of such pattern in certain ferroelectrics has been attributed to the external screening effect from mobile charge defects or free car

